WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.289
A program is stored instructions; a process is an executing instance with state and resources.

00:00:05.289 --> 00:00:09.873
A process may contain multiple threads that share parts of its address space.

00:00:09.873 --> 00:00:14.810
Running, ready and blocked describe whether execution currently has CPU time or is waiting.

00:00:15.810 --> 00:00:19.887
The requesting thread can block while another ready task uses the CPU.

00:00:19.887 --> 00:00:26.002
When the data arrives, the waiting thread becomes ready; it may still wait for scheduling before running again.

00:00:26.002 --> 00:00:28.040
Ready does not mean currently running.

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:33.257
An I/O completion usually moves a blocked task into which state?

00:00:33.257 --> 00:00:38.625
The task becomes eligible to run; the scheduler determines when it gets CPU time.
